<h1>Closing Thoughts</h1>
<p>While the behavior of "Open in Emacs" seems modest, a <em>lot</em> of things need to happen under the hood to orchestrate the different apps required to make it happen on macOS. But taking a step back, I'd observe that this is not unique to just macOS alone. Other contemporary operating systems are following suit as seen with <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snap_(software)">Snap (Ubuntu)</a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flatpak">Flatpak</a> for Linux, and UWP for Windows. Such isolating practices by OS vendors are <em>not</em> going away.</p>
<p>Running counter to this is the spate of interest in orchestrating apps and/or services together using AI tools, whose approach so far is to bypass all hardened runtime boundaries.</p>
<p>Then there is comparison to the Emacs ecosystem, where orchestration of apps (or rather modes) is routinely done via Elisp. By implementation circumstance there are effectively no concerns for security boundaries within the internals of Emacs.</p>
<p>There's a lot of ground to ruminate on, in particular software malleability and orchestration, which I intend to do with future posts on this blog.</p>